## Article

```catala
declaration scope A:
  input x content integer
  input y content integer
  output o content integer

scope A:
    definition o equals 0
    exception definition o under condition y = 0 consequence equals 1
    exception definition o under condition x/y = 0.5 consequence equals 2
```

Here we can see that when the first exception condition is true, the division
by zero error is always raised and we don't try to find a way (that does not
exist) to get a conflict.
Internally, generating such inputs would require solving for 2 constraints :
```
    y = 0 (from first exception)
and y! = 0 (from division by y in second exception)
```
which is unsatisfiable.

```catala-test-cli
$ catala Concolic --conc-optim=incremental -s A
┌─[WARNING]─
│
│  Output of scope after evaluation:
│    ↯DivisionByZero↯ at div_error_before_conflict:12.44-47
│      Null denominator:
│      ─➤ tests/concolic/good/div_error_before_conflict.catala_en:12.46-47:
│         │
│      12 │     exception definition o under condition x/y = 0.5 consequence equals 2
│         │                                              ‾
│      Article
│
└─
┌─[RESULT]─ Concolic execution ─
│ Executed 3 tests:
│     Inputs │ Outputs
│ ────── Test #1 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
│ x_in = 0   │ ↯DivisionByZero↯ at div_error_before_conflict:12.44-47
│ y_in = 0   │   Null denominator:
│            │   ─➤ tests/concolic/good/div_error_before_conflict.catala_en:12.46-47:
│            │      │
│            │   12 │     exception definition o under condition x/y = 0.5 consequence equals 2
│            │      │                                              ‾
│            │   Article
│ ────── Test #2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
│ x_in = 0   │ o = 0
│ y_in = 2   │
│ ────── Test #3 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
│ x_in = -1  │ o = 2
│ y_in = -2  │
└─
```


